{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "Circuit-only project: a complete linear power supply driving a DC motor. V1 (12 V RMS, 50 Hz) feeds the bridge rectifier D1-D4; C1 smooths the pulsating DC to about 15 V with a few hundred mV of ripple (probe RAW). Behind the 1.6 A slow-blow fuse F1 the 7809 regulator U1 turns it into a clean 9 V motor rail (voltmeter VM1, buffered by C2 which also absorbs the energy the motor returns), from which the 7805 U2 makes the 5 V logic rail with the POWER LED. The L293-style H-bridge U3 switches the motor M1 between the 9 V rail and ground: the DIR switch puts logic high on IN1 or IN2 (R2 / R3 pull the other input low) to choose the direction, and the 200 Hz clock on the enable input chops the drive at 75 % duty (PWM speed control); between pulses the motor current free-wheels through the clamp diodes of the bridge. Flip DIR to brake and reverse the motor, stop the PWM clock by clicking it, or load the motor (load torque) and watch the current rise - the 7809 limits at 1.5 A, and a sustained current above about 2.1 A blows the fuse; click it to replace it.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Regulated Power Supply with Motor Drive"
  },
